3}f(x).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(1,2)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,3\\cdot (2))=(1,6).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(1,6)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,-(6))=(1,-6).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $4$ units down and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)-4.$ After this transformation, the point $(1,-6)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(1,-6-4)=(1,-10).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(1,2)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{3}f(x),$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}f(1)=2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad f(1)=6.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)-4.$ Substituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y&=-f(1)-4\\\\&=-(6)-4\\\\&=-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,-10)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)-4.$" 13194139535560 17592186045443 true] [17592186517134 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517132 13194139535560 17592186045443 true] [17592186517134 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535560 17592186045443 true] [17592186517134 1071 "The point $(-7,8)$ lies on the curve $y=2f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)-4?$" 13194139535560 17592186045443 true] [17592186517134 1072 #uuid "b988807a-34eb-52af-995a-9e3c8a1f0e2f" 13194139535560 17592186045443 true] [17592186517134 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(-7,8)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)-4:$\n\n- First, take the graph $y=2f(x).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-7,8)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-7,\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (8))=(-7,4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-7,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-7,-(4))=(-7,-4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $4$ units down and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)-4.$ After this transformation, the point $(-7,-4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-7,-4-4)=(-7,-8).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-7,8)$ lies on the graph of $y=2f(x),$ we have that\n\n$$\n2f(-7)=8\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad f(-7)=4.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)-4.$ Substituting $x=-7,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y&=-f(-7)-4\\\\&=-(4)-4\\\\&=-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-7,-8)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)-4.$" 13194139535560 17592186045443 true] [17592186517135 1080 "(-7,16)" 13194139535560 17592186045443 true] [17592186517135 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535560 17592186045443 true] [17592186517135 1083 #uuid "d79a5116-a0ab-50fa-a0de-8d76eaba32ca" 13194139535560 17592186045443 true] [17592186517136 1080 "(1,10)" 13194139535560 17592186045443 true] [17592186517136 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535560 17592186045443 true] [17592186517136 1083 #uuid "d8770e35-e2a3-50d5-be73-804751858bee" 13194139535560 17592186045443 true] [17592186517137 1080 "(1,6)" 13194139535560 17592186045443 true] [17592186517137 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535560 17592186045443 true] [17592186517137 1083 #uuid "e3d7550c-e25d-5ef7-964e-f5fa32a57502" 13194139535560 17592186045443 true] [17592186517138 1080 "(-8,\\frac{27}{2})" 13194139535560 17592186045443 true] [17592186517138 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535560 17592186045443 true] [17592186517138 1083 #uuid "e6f14205-061b-58e6-8ec2-15a66df7a546" 13194139535560 17592186045443 true] [17592186517139 1080 "(1,\\frac{1}{3})" 13194139535560 17592186045443 true] [17592186517139 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535560 17592186045443 true] [17592186517139 1083 #uuid "f7dbd166-b5b2-5ec2-a66a-c579223ce6ae" 13194139535560 17592186045443 true] [17592186517140 1068 #edb/ref 17592186517128 13194139535560 17592186045443 true] [17592186517140 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535560 17592186045443 true] [17592186517140 1071 "The point $(-8,7)$ lies on the curve $y=4f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)-5?$" 13194139535560 17592186045443 true] [17592186517140 1072 #uuid "f8cb0f82-4ced-5ad3-8499-c17a38448a7a" 13194139535560 17592186045443 true] [17592186517140 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(-8,7)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)-5:$\n\n- First, take the graph $y=4f(x).$\n\n- Then, we stretch the graph by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{4}$ parallel to the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-8,7)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-8,\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (7))=(-8,\\frac{7}{4}).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(-8,\\frac{7}{4})$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-8,-(\\frac{7}{4}))=(-8,- \\frac{7}{4}).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $5$ units down and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)-5.$ After this transformation, the point $(-8,- \\frac{7}{4})$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-8,- \\frac{7}{4}-5)=(-8,- \\frac{27}{4}).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(-8,7)$ lies on the graph of $y=4f(x),$ we have that\n\n$$\n4f(-8)=7\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad f(-8)=\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)-5.$ Substituting $x=-8,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y&=-f(-8)-5\\\\&=-(\\frac{7}{4})-5\\\\&=- \\frac{27}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-8,- \\frac{27}{4})$ must lie on $y=-f(x)-5.$" 13194139535560 17592186045443 true] [17592186517141 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186059669.py" 13194139535560 17592186045443 true] [17592186517141 1169 #uuid "bd32fee7-3163-5e9a-a7e1-90f7b06d904d" 13194139535560 17592186045443 true]] :edb/tempids {"11b3afc9-f9cc-5c8a-b6ab-4390c55f86f1" 17592186517120 "2d286aa7-b2d6-58d2-bf29-61fb1720d50e